ap. The wrapper holds a reference and\n  uses only `db.table('_default')`. It does not modify the instance, its\n  storage, or any other table.\n- No validation of `db` beyond attribute access at use time (the wrapper is\n  a thin, non-invasive adapter; R03).\n\n### `apply(operations) -> list[int]`\n\n**Parameters**\n\n- `operations`: a `list` of operation `dict`s (see [data-model.md](../data-model.md)).\n\n**Returns**\n\n- `list[int]`: the IDs of documents inserted by this batch, in insertion\n  order. `remove` operations add no result. `apply([])` returns `[]`.\n\n**Behavior**\n\n1. **Validation (no side effects)**: `operations` must be a `list`; each op\n   must be a `dict` with `op` in `{'insert', 'remove'}`; `insert` requires\n   `document` to be a `dict`; `remove` requires `doc_id` to be an `int` with\n   `doc_id > 0` (bools rejected). Any violation raises `ValueError` and the\n   default table is left untouched.\n2. **Snapshot**: the complete pre-batch default-table data is deep-copied.\n3. **Sequential application**: ops are applied in order via the ordinary\n   `Table` API; inserted documents are deep-copied before storage (no\n   aliasing of caller input, R03).\n4. **Atomicity**: if any op fails at apply time — including a `remove` whose\n   `doc_id` does not exist in the table at that point (raises `ValueError`) —\n   the default table is restored to the exact pre-batch snapshot and the\n   error propagates. Previously acquired `db.table('_default')` handles\n   observe both success and rollback (they read storage per access).\n\n**Errors**\n\n| Condition | Exception |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| `operations` is not a list | `ValueError` |\n| an op is not a dict | `ValueError` |\n| `op` missing or not `'insert'`/`'remove'` | `ValueError` |\n| `insert` missing `document`, or `document` not a dict | `ValueError` |\n| `remove` missing `doc_id`, or `doc_id` not a positive int (bools rejected) | `ValueError` |\n| `remove` targets a missing `doc_id` at apply time | `ValueError` (batch rolled back) |\n| any unexpected error during apply | propagated (batch rolled back) |\n\n**Non-goals (out of contract)**\n\n- No updates, no multi-table batches, no nested/transactional composition,\n  no concurrency guarantees (A5/A7).\n- `apply` does not return stored documents, only inserted IDs.\n\n**Compatibility**\n\n- Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes on the wrapped instance behave exactly as\n  before wrapping; preexisting documents remain accessible (R03, FR-008).\n- Mutating caller-supplied operation dicts (or their nested values) after\n  `apply` — success or failure — cannot mutate the database (R03, FR-009).\nEOF\ncat > specs/001-transactions/quickstart.md <<'EOF'\n# Quickstart / Validation Guide: BatchWriter\n\n**Phase 1 output** | **Date**: 2026-10-17 | **Plan**: [plan.md](plan.md)\n\nRunnable validation scenarios proving the feature works end-to-end. Contract\ndetails: [contracts/batchwriter.md](contracts/batchwriter.md). Model details:\n[data-model.md](data-model.md).\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- Python 3.10+ (repo `requires-python = \">=3.10,<4\"`); environment observed on\n  Python 3.12.14 with pytest 9.1.1.\n- `tinydb/journal.py` implemented (milestone deliverable).\n- No new dependencies. `pytest-cov` is NOT installed here; always run pytest\n  with `-o addopts=\"\"` to bypass the repo `pytest.ini` coverage flags.\n\n## Setup\n\n```sh\ncd /testbed\n# PYTHONPATH is not required (repo root is the source root), but if you run\n# from elsewhere use: export PYTHONPATH=src:.  (repo convention)\n```\n\n## Scenario 1 — Basic apply (R01)\n\n```python\nfrom tinydb import TinyDB\nfrom tinydb.storages import MemoryStorage\nfrom tinydb.journal import BatchWriter\n\ndb = TinyDB(storage=MemoryStorage)\ndb.insert({'x': 1})                 # preexisting doc, id 1\nw = BatchWriter(db)\n\nassert w.apply([]) == []            # empty batch\nids = w.apply([\n    {'op': 'insert', 'document': {'x': 2, 'nested': {'a': [1, 2]}}},\n    {'op': 'remove', 